    "body": "Sorry, you are running into a few subtle nuances here:\r\n\r\n1. `:path*` is not a \"named wildcard\".  There is an overloading of the `*` character.  When there is a grouping construct in front of the `*` it functions as the \"zero or more\" modifier like in regular expressions.\r\n2. When there is an \"optional\" modifier like the \"zero or more\" `*` modifier, the \"prefix\" of the group is also made optional along with.\r\n3. In pathname a `/` before the group is automatically treated as a prefix.\r\n\r\nSo in your example the second pattern does not match both inputs because the pattern is equivalent to `http://a/b{/:path}*`.  This fails to match the `/a/b/` input because there is a slash, but no trailing characters.  The pattern requires them to either both be present or missing.\r\n\r\nTo get the pattern to match both inputs you can change the pattern to use explicit `{}` grouping to avoid the automatic prefix behavior.   So try this:\r\n\r\n`http://a/b/{:path}*`\r\n\r\nOr you can instead use the regex value that matches the wildcard behavior instead of the \"zero or more modifier\":\r\n\r\n`http://a/b/:path(.*)`\r\n\r\nSorry for the confusion.  Hope that helps.",
